## Importing Names into Scope

The `use` statement brings module items into the current scope, so you can call them without the module prefix.

### Three Import Styles

```xiom
module math {
  pub fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { return a + b; }
  pub fn multiply(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { return a * b; }
}

// Style 1: Import the whole module
use math;

fn main() -> Int {
  let sum = math.add(3, 4);  // Still need prefix
  io.println(string.int_to_str(sum));
  return 0;
}
```

Wait — `use math;` still requires the prefix? Let's explore:

```xiom
// Style 2: Import specific items (no prefix needed)
// Syntax: use module.item;

use math.add;
use math.multiply;

fn main() -> Int {
  let sum = add(3, 4);        // No prefix!
  let prod = multiply(5, 6);  // No prefix!
  io.println(string.int_to_str(sum));
  return 0;
}
```

### When to Use Each Style

| Style | Syntax | Prefix Needed | Best For |
|-------|--------|--------------|----------|
| Full path | `module.item()` | Yes (always) | Occasional use, clarity |
| Module import | `use module;` then `module.item()` | Yes | Multiple items from one module |
| Item import | `use module.item;` | No | Frequently used single items |

### Practical Example

```xiom
module io_utils {
  pub fn print_label(label: Str, value: Int) {
    io.println(label + ": " + string.int_to_str(value));
  }

  pub fn print_header(title: Str) {
    io.println("=== " + title + " ===");
  }
}

use io_utils.print_label;
use io_utils.print_header;

fn main() -> Int {
  print_header("Report");
  print_label("Count", 42);
  return 0;
}
```

### The Tradeoff

Prefixes like `io_utils.print_label` make it obvious where functions come from — great for large codebases. Imports like `use io_utils.print_label;` make code shorter — great for frequently used functions. Choose based on clarity vs convenience.

